  "body_html": "<h1>Strictly Competitive Perfect-Information Determinacy</h1>\n<p>Every finite strictly competitive game with perfect information is determined.</p>\n<p>Equivalently, when terminal outcomes are interpreted as player 1 payoffs and\nplayer 2 has the opposite preference order, the game has a pure-strategy value.</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Sketch</p>\n<p>Order outcomes by player 1's preference. Let $R_k$ be the smallest initial segment\nof outcomes that player 1 can guarantee. Player 1 can guarantee $R_k$, while by\nZermelo determinacy player 2 can guarantee the complement of $R_{k-1}$. This\nidentifies the threshold outcome and gives determinacy.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Cor. 6.2.4] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Every finite strictly competitive perfect-information game is determined.</li>\n</ul>",
